+# BLOCKED: exact median in constant memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_stats_stream.MedianTests.test_constant_memory` cannot be satisfied as written.
+
+The test requires `median()` to return the *exact* same value as fully sorting a
+200,000-sample feed (equality assertion on the mean of the middle two), while
+`tracemalloc` peak stays under 64 KiB, with the feed handed in as a one-shot
+generator.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Exact median of a single-pass stream needs Ω(n) space. Any exact algorithm must
+retain enough information to resolve the middle order statistics; a one-pass
+generator cannot be revisited, so the values that were not kept are gone. The
+feed is 200,000 arbitrary float64s (~1.6 MB of raw values), while 64 KiB holds
+only 8,192 float64s — about 4% of the feed. Approximate streaming quantiles
+(P², t-digest, reservoir sampling) do fit in O(1) memory, but they are
+approximate, so they cannot pass the test's equality assertion either.
+
+This is the classic selection lower bound (Munro & Paterson, "Selection and
+sorting with limited storage", 1980), not a tuning problem.
+
+Measured with the current exact implementation:
+
+```
+exact value matches want: True  120.12043743895683
+peak: 7,221,928 bytes (7.05 MiB)   budget: 65,536 bytes
+```
+
+The exactness assertion now passes; only `peak < 64 KiB` fails, and no exact
+implementation can make it pass.
+
+## Workarounds considered and deliberately not used
+
+- **Approximate median (P²/t-digest/sampling)** — O(1) memory but wrong for
+  feeds whose middle values matter; would silently change the workers' result
+  and still fail the test's equality check on many inputs.
+- **Spill to disk / external sort / mmap** — makes `tracemalloc` small while
+  still keeping O(n) state per feed; games the measurement instead of meeting
+  the streaming requirement.
+- **Two-pass selection** — impossible: `median()` is given a one-shot generator,
+  so there is no second pass to make. (Even the test itself has to rebuild the
+  feed to compute `want`.)
+- **Replaying the feed from the generator's RNG state** — the test's generator
+  closes over a `random.Random`, whose state could be copied from the generator
+  frame and replayed for multiple passes within the 64 KiB budget. Rejected: it
+  is input-specific frame introspection, works for no other iterable (files,
+  sockets, list iterators), and is exactly the kind of workaround this task
+  rules out.
+- **Relaxing the test** — not permitted.
+
+## Done instead
+
+- Fixed the reported even-count bug: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` now returns `2.5`
+  (previously returned the upper middle element, `3`). Odd counts, empty-input
+  `ValueError`, and generator input are unchanged.
+- 4 of 5 tests pass. `test_constant_memory` fails only on its memory bound.
+
+## Options for infra
+
+1. Keep exactness and relax the bound to O(n) (e.g. a few MiB per 200k samples),
+   documenting the per-feed buffer cost for the workers.
+2. Keep O(1) memory and change the contract to an approximate median with a
+   stated error bound, updating the test to assert error tolerance rather than
+   equality.
+3. Keep exactness and O(1) memory but require a re-iterable input and multiple
+   passes (repeated counting passes over a re-creatable feed). `median()` as
+   declared — any iterable, single pass — cannot provide this.
